Dear Simon,

thank you for your reply. It works now!

One part of the solution was to remove a line of code in the method initR(). It was a duplicate to actionPerformed(ActionEvent e):
_re.eval("JavaGD()"); 


> Based on the behavior is seems that your custom class is not used. .setenv() is not a valid function in R - there is Sys.setenv() which is presumably what you meant? 

This was the most important part to get it run: I changed the code to „Sys.sentenv()“:

	_re.eval("Sys.setenv('JAVAGD_CLASS_NAME'='moneytrax/MyJavaGD2')");

Then i moved the class „MyJavaGD2“ out of the class „Test_UI_RAnalyzeOutput“ into a separate class file "MyJavaGD2.java“. This was the only way to set the JAVAGD_CLASS_NAME correctly. I tried a lot of variations like "moneytrax/Test_UI_RAnalyzeOutput.MyJavaGD2“ but nothing worked. I always got the famous exception: 

	Exception in thread "AWT-EventQueue-0“ java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError

My explanation is that JAVAGD_CLASS_NAME needs a special path syntax. Could you please comment on this?

> Some of the code isn't really needed - e.g. you should not manually resize anything - either set the right size in the call to JavaGD() or let the even handling run the resize event.

Yes, you’re right. The canvas resizes automatically when changing the size of the window. But when you resize the window and push afterwards the plot button, the canvas needs a refresh:

	_gdc.initRefresh(); 

Could you please explain why?

A general question: Do you think it is possible to put a general example into the package of JavaGD? There seems to be a lot of confusion out there.

Best regards,
Bastian

